{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"title","type":"text","text":"Module 11 — Enterprise Virtual Network Security\nMgmt Evolution → Virtualization Concepts → NV → SDN → NFV → Containers → Container Hardening → Docker → Kubernetes","x":-1180,"y":-3560,"width":2360,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"b1","type":"text","text":"LO01 · Security Management in Virtualization-Enabled IT Environments\n\nTRADITIONAL MODEL\nevaluation → selection → procurement → installation → configuration → maintaining security\n\nWHY IT BROKE\ngeographic expansion · dynamic throughput (commerce/media/voice/mobile/IoT) · protracted procurement cycles · cannot control resources or enforce policy · increasingly disaggregated networks · limited dynamic provisioning/de-provisioning and policy modification\n\nSHIFT → SDN + NFV\nflexibility · centralized control · fine-grained policy · data/app protection · network automation · efficiency","x":-2000,"y":-3400,"width":480,"height":330,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"b2","type":"text","text":"LO02 · Essential Virtualization Concepts\n\nDEF software-based virtual representation; many virtual resources from one physical resource or vice versa\n\nAPPROACHES — who translates?\nFull → VMM (guest unaware)\nPara/OS-assisted → guest OS (VMM NOT involved)\nHardware-assisted → CPU special instructions\nHybrid → guest para + VMM translation\n\nLEVELS (where) · Storage Device · File System · Server · Fabric (SAN)\nTYPES (what) · Operating System · Network · Server · Desktop\n\nTRAP Server Virtualization is in BOTH lists\n\nCOMPONENTS hypervisor/VMM · guest · host · mgmt server · mgmt console · network components · virtual storage\nENABLERS NV · SDN · NFV — decouple control and forwarding planes","x":-2000,"y":-2960,"width":480,"height":420,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"b3","type":"text","text":"LO03 · Network Virtualization (NV) Security  (46 pp, 30 figures)\n\nCONCEPTS share all network resources under ONE administrative unit · abstract hardware into software · split bandwidth into independent channels reassigned in real time\n\nPLACEMENT external (outside a virtual server) vs internal (inside) — decided by platform size/type; internal network-in-a-box = containers + hypervisor control programs + vNICs\n\nTHREAT CLASSES (reused) · Disclosure · Deception · Disruption · Usurpation\n\nATTACKS\nHypervisor → VM escape chain\nVirtual network → DoS via software-controlled latency, reply attacks by misbehaving virtual routers, uncontrolled resource allocation, improper validation\nVLAN → MAC flooding · ARP attack · DHCP starvation · multicast brute-force\n2 VLAN-HOPPING PATHS → trunk-port spoofing emulating 802.1Q + DTP | STP root election via topology-change-ack BPDUs\n\nSECURITY\nHyper-V: time sync · access privileges · disable unnecessary services · IUM · SMB 3.0 · Hyper-V Administrators group\nVMware · VirtualBox\nzoning (DMZ) · SELinux · sVirt · hidepid · GRSecurity · hypervisor introspection","x":-2000,"y":-2430,"width":480,"height":560,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"b4","type":"text","text":"LO04 · Software-Defined Network (SDN) Security\n\nSDN an approach to network management differing from traditional; consistent management network-wide; logically centralized control vs distributed; central view; manage physical AND virtual switches; SDN APIs; whitelist model; QoS for VoIP/multimedia\n\nTABLE 11.1 SDN-specific vulnerabilities — credentials, policy discovery by timing analysis, flow-rule modification, controller-switch comms, switch flow-table flooding, no TLS, no secure provisioning, no network-state visibility, data modification, DoS, config issues\n\nATTACKS BY COMPONENT IMPACTED\nDATA PLANE — exactly 3\n  Device Attack → switch software/hardware, firmware, TCAM\n  Protocol Attack → forwarding-device protocol vulns\n  Side Channel Attack → deduce forwarding policy\nSouthbound API · Control Plane · Northbound · Application Plane\n\nMEASURES\napp → controller authentication + encryption · secure coding northbound · TLS device/agent/controller · nonces vs replay\nSNMPv3 not SNMPv2c · SSH not telnet · DCI tunnel auth · OOB network for control traffic","x":-1400,"y":-2960,"width":480,"height":470,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"b5","type":"text","text":"LO05 · Network Function Virtualization (NFV) Security\n\n3 PRINCIPAL ELEMENTS\nNFVI infrastructure · VNF virtualized network functions · MANO management and orchestration\n\nATTACKS\nprogrammable NIC cards · virtual switch partly IN the hypervisor → trap or generate malicious packets\nResource Freeing Attacks (RFA) · Resource Consumption Attacks\nmalicious NaaS providers → DoS + secret extraction\nSIDE CHANNEL attacker VM extracts a private ElGamal decryption key from a co-resident victim VM running GnuPG\n\nMEASURES\nNFVI → hypervisor domain: authn controlled by the VMs | compute domain: encrypt, access only by sharing VNFs | network domain: secure networking techniques\nMANO → automate security at policy enforcement points (PEPs) · controller availability (central decision point) · orchestrator attack → user authn, privilege control, monitoring to separate defective VNFs · storage protection · scaling/elasticity\nBest practices → secure packet processing system monitoring instruction-level operations of the packet processor","x":-1400,"y":-2380,"width":480,"height":420,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"b6","type":"text","text":"LO06 · OS Virtualization Security  (= CONTAINERS, not Hyper-V)\n\nDEF host OS kernel virtually replicated in multiple instances of isolated user space, called containers, software containers, or virtualization engines\n\nCaaS · orchestration (automated lifecycle + dynamic environment) — Docker Swarm · OpenShift · Kubernetes\n\nCONTAINER vs VM (Table 11.2)\ncontainer = OS-level, lightweight, share host OS, process-level isolation, less memory | LXC LXD CGManager Docker\nVM = hardware-level, heavyweight, own OS each | VMware Hyper-V vSphere VirtualBox\n\nDOCKER NETWORKING\nCNM Sandbox/Endpoint/Network\nnative drivers H·B·O·MAC·N = Host · Bridge · Overlay · MACVLAN · None\nremote drivers (community/vendors) · IPAM drivers\n\nKUBERNETES ARCHITECTURE\ncontrol plane kube-apiserver · etcd · kube-scheduler · kube-controller-manager · cloud-controller-manager (-cloud-provider external)\nnode kubelet · kube-proxy · container runtime (Docker, CRI-O, CRI)\n7 features service discovery · load balancing · storage orchestration · rollouts/rollbacks · bin packing · self-healing · secret/config mgmt\n\n12 SECURITY CHALLENGES · risks by image/registry/orchestrator/container/host OS\nATTACKS escaping · cross-container · inner-container · registry · data exfiltration (reverse shell to C2, tunneling) · cryptomining · scanning","x":-1400,"y":-1850,"width":480,"height":560,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"b7","type":"text","text":"LO07 · Containers — Guidelines & Best Practices\n\nHARDENING configured against benchmarks · control features for host/daemon/kernel · avoid privileged modes\n\nIMAGE SECURITY\ntrusted registry · know the base image · reduce attack surface\n\nRUNTIME SECURITY\n\nSECRETS — 8 RULES\nnever environment variables\nnever bake secrets into images\nlog all secret operations\ntransfer over a secure channel\nencrypt secrets, decrypt with the container private key\nthird-party secret stores\nrotate periodically\nrevoke immediately if exposed\n\nCLOSING BEST PRACTICES\ndo not trust a container's software · know what happens inside each container · control root access · check the container runtime · lock down the OS · secure containers supporting microservices · trusted registry · reduce attack surface · vulnerability assessment process · isolation and least privilege · centrally managed access controls · real-time threat detection and IR\n\nCites NIST recommendations","x":-800,"y":-2960,"width":480,"height":470,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"b8","type":"text","text":"LO08 · Dockers — Guidelines & Best Practices\n\nDOCKER SECURITY FEATURES (37 Linux mechanisms; 14 implemented by Trusted Content)\nCgroups · LSMs · Capabilities · Seccomp · Userns\n\nDOCKER CONTENT TRUST (DCT)\nsudo export DOCKER_CONTENT_TRUST=1\n\nRESOURCE LIMITS\ndefault = NO limit; can use as much as the host scheduler provides\n--cpus=2 limits to 2 CPUs; memory option limits to 1 GB\nunlimited → OOM\n\nTOOLS\nDocker Bench Security (CIS-derived checks)\nStackRox — www.stackrox.com — full life cycle build/deploy/runtime; process execution, network connections and flows, privilege escalation, files launched per container in Kubernetes; policies detect crypto mining, privilege escalation, exploits\n+ named commercial tools (Falco, Sysdig, Aqua, Black Duck, Tenable Flawcheck, …)\n\nBEST PRACTICES\nmulti-stage builds → cut image-dependency attack surface\nhadolint linter → resolve Dockerfile issues (printed pp127 and 129)","x":-800,"y":-2380,"width":480,"height":420,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"b9","type":"text","text":"LO09 · Kubernetes — Guidelines & Best Practices\n\nRBAC WITH LEAST PRIVILEGE, DISABLE ABAC\nRBAC API blocks privilege escalation by editing roles/role bindings; enforced AT THE API LEVEL even when the RBAC authorizer is not in use\nSCOPE RULE may create/update a role only if holding ALL its permissions AT THE SAME SCOPE\nClusterRole = cluster-wide · Role = same namespace\napiGroup rbac.authorization.k8s.io\n\nAUDIT POLICY\nlog all requests at the Metadata level (apiVersion audit.k8s.io/v1, kind Policy)\n\nNETWORK POLICIES\npods are NON-ISOLATED BY DEFAULT\npolicies are ADDITIVE → union of rules\nserver > v1.8 · provider must support policy (Calico, Cilium, Kube-router, Romana)\nplayground Minikube · Katacoda · Play with Kubernetes\n\nPodSecurityPolicy (extensions/v1beta1)\nprivileged false · runAsUser RunAsNonRoot · restrict Linux capabilities · restrict volumes to block NFS · block host ports\n\nSECRETS\nnever a config file\nproviders identity · aescbc · secretbox · aesgcm · kms\naesgcm = AES-GCM random nonce, ENVELOPE encryption (DEK under AES-CBC PKCS#7, wrapped by KEK)\nhead -c 32 /dev/urandom | base 64\nverify with etcdctl; stored secret prefixed k8s:enc:aescbc\n\nTOOLS Istio (istio.io, service mesh, mTLS pod-to-pod) · Grafeas (software supply chain audit)\nCIS Benchmark · keep up to date","x":-800,"y":-1850,"width":480,"height":560,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"pat","type":"text","text":"THE REPEATING SPINE\n(LO03–LO06 each run concept → vulnerability/attack → security measure)\n\nNV concepts → NV attacks → NV security\nSDN concepts → SDN attacks → SDN measures\nNFV NFVI/VNF/MANO → NFV attacks → NFV measures\nContainers concepts → challenges/risks → attacks → hardening\n\n\nVIRTUAL-NETWORK THREAT CLASSES recur for BOTH the hypervisor and the virtual network:\nDisclosure · Deception · Disruption · Usurpation\n\n\nLOAD-BEARING EXAM ANCHORS\nwho translates (Full/Para/Hardware/Hybrid)\nlevels vs types\nSDN Data Plane = exactly 3 attacks\nRBAC scope rule\npods non-isolated by default; policies additive\naesgcm envelope encryption\nLO06 is CONTAINERS — Hyper-V is LO03","x":200,"y":-2960,"width":520,"height":700,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"end","type":"text","text":"MODULE SUMMARY (pp156–157)\n\nVirtualization = software-based virtual representation of an IT infrastructure: network, devices, applications, storage\nNV, SDN and NFV are the technologies that enable virtualization\nOS virtualization replicates kernel functionality into containers, software containers, or virtualization engines\nTime synchronization, access privileges, disabling unnecessary services, isolating user mode and encrypting guest VMs protect VMs\nSecure NFV infrastructure → secure packet processing system monitoring instruction-level packet-processor operations\nContainer hardening secures images, runtime, secrets and registry\nDocker = open source tech for developing, packaging and running applications with all dependencies as containers\nKubernetes (K8s) = open-source, portable, extensible orchestration platform\nPod Security Policies = central cluster-level configuration and security controls","x":840,"y":-2600,"width":520,"height":480,"color":"4"}
	],
